ture and prevalence of hantavirus infection in rodents: a geographic division of the enzootic area in far eastern Russia</article-title><source>J Vector Ecol</source><year>1997</year><volume>22</volume><fpage>52</fpage><lpage>63</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9221739</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B7"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Githeko</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Lindsay</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Confalonieri</surname><given-names>U</given-names></name><name><surname>Patz</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Climate change and vector-borne diseases: a regional analysis</article-title><source>B World Health Organ</source><year>2000</year><volume>78</volume><fpage>1136</fpage><lpage>1147</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B8"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hales</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Wet</surname><given-names>Nd</given-names></name><name><surname>Maindonald</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Woodward</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Potential effect of population and climate change on global distribution of dengue fever: an empirical model</article-title><source>Lancet</source><year>2002</year><volume>360</volume><fpage>830</fpage><lpage>834</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12243917</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0140-6736(02)09964-6</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B9"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hopp</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Foley</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Global-scale relationships between climate and the dengue fever vector, <italic>Aedes aegypti</italic></article-title><source>Climatic